How would you implement a data pipeline in Azure Databricks to process and analyze streaming data from IoT devices, ensuring scalability and fault tolerance?

To process and analyze streaming IoT data in Azure Databricks, I would build a scalable pipeline using Azure IoT Hub/Event Hubs, Structured Streaming, and Delta Lake.
First, IoT devices send telemetry data such as temperature, pressure, and device status to Azure IoT Hub or Event Hubs. 
Databricks consumes these events in real time using Structured Streaming and a dedicated consumer group to isolate the workload.
The incoming data is validated, filtered, and enriched with reference data such as device metadata, location information, or device type. I would then perform real-time aggregations, such as calculating average temperature per device or detecting anomalies using window-based operations.
To handle varying event volumes, I would enable autoscaling on Databricks clusters so that additional workers are added automatically when throughput increases.
For fault tolerance, I would configure checkpointing. Checkpoints store offsets and streaming progress so that if a node or cluster fails, the stream resumes from the last successfully processed offset without data loss
Finally, I would store the processed data in Delta Lake. Delta Lake provides ACID transactions, schema enforcement, time travel, and exactly-once write guarantees, making it suitable for both real-time dashboards and downstream analytics workloads."
