Describe the process of integrating Azure Databricks with Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 for a data lake solution.

To integrate Azure Databricks with Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 (ADLS Gen2),
I first configure secure authentication using either a Service Principal or Managed Identity
. I then grant the required permissions on the storage account through Azure RBAC, 
typically using the Storage Blob Data Contributor role.
Once authentication is configured, Databricks can access the storage either 
through mounted paths or directly using ABFSS URLs. In modern implementations, 
direct ABFSS access is preferred because it provides better security, governance, and Unity Catalog integration.
After connectivity is established, I use Delta Lake tables on top of ADLS Gen2 to store data. 
Delta Lake provides ACID transactions, schema enforcement, time travel, and optimized metadata management.
This combination of Databricks and ADLS Gen2 forms the foundation of a scalable lakehouse architecture
